  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{\u2032}=y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$\n- $(\\pi ,-1)$\n- $(0,-1)$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{\u2032}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{\u2032}=y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{\u2032}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,\u2713 \\\\ {{y}^{\u2032}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{\u2032}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
